  32	The forward half --- a universal divisor family on \(\mathbf{Div}\) --- is not built here.
  33	Section~\ref{sec:divrep} states a conditional package for it and isolates the unproved
  34	statement (\ref{thm:divRepUniversalFamily}).  It does not by itself produce the
  35	open-immersion certificates or the local-surjectivity instance consumed by
  36	\ref{def:pic0RepresentableByOfCharts}; those are separate hypotheses at the representability
  37	seam.
  38	
  39	The finite two-chart presentation in this chapter is an auxiliary window engine.  The divisor
  40	functor used in the representability argument instead permits certificates on arbitrary
  41	affine-open covers of the relative curve.  Thus the fixed chart cover data below is not the
  42	definition of that widened functor.
  43	
  44	Throughout, \(k\) is a field, \(C\) is a smooth proper geometrically irreducible curve over
  45	\(k\) of genus \(g\), \(\pi \colon C \to \PP^1_k\) is a finite dominant morphism, and
  46	\(F\) is its fibre Weil divisor (\ref{def:fiberWeilDivisor}). For a \(k\)-algebra \(R\) we
  47	write \(C_R\) for the relative curve (\ref{def:relCurve}) and \(V_0^R, V_1^R\) for the two
  48	pinned charts of \(C_R\) obtained by pulling back the two standard charts of \(\PP^1\)
  49	along \(\pi\) (\ref{def:relCover}, \ref{def:fiberTwoCover}). We abbreviate
  50	\(H_a := H^0(C, \OO(aF))\) (\ref{def:divisorSections}).  These pinned charts occur only
  51	in the auxiliary window calculations.  Kleiman's Main theorem, which is cited below, asks
  52	that the morphism be projective (Zariski locally on the base), flat, and have integral
  53	geometric fibres (\ref{thm:divRepUniversalFamily}); those hypotheses are not synonyms for
  54	smooth, proper, and geometrically irreducible, and require an explicit bridge.
