  57	\label{sec:diveq}
  58	
  59	A local-equation system (\ref{def:localEquations}) is a presentation of an effective
  60	Cartier divisor, not the divisor itself: the same divisor is presented by many systems,
 132	Fix a \(k\)-algebra \(R\). A relative effective divisor on \(C_R/R\) is, locally on
 133	\(C_R\), cut out by one equation which is regular on the fibres; a local-equation system on
 134	\(C_R\) records exactly that, but on an \emph{arbitrary} pointed cover, and with regularity
 135	asked in the stalks of \(C_R\) rather than on the fibres.  For the auxiliary pinned-window
 136	presentation, a refinement by finitely many basic opens of the two pinned charts is paired
 137	with a certificate asserting that the resulting colength modules are finite projective over
 138	\(R\) of constant rank \(n\).  The widened divisor carrier used by the representability route
 139	is a separate quotient in which each local certificate is carried by an arbitrary affine-open
 140	cover of the relative curve.  The \(\Div^{\,n}_{\mathrm{Zar}}\) carrier defined below retains
 141	the pinned-window certificate and is used only for the auxiliary comparison.
 142	In either presentation the certificate is the proposed formal
 143	analogue of \(R\)-flatness with fibres of degree \(n\) in Kleiman's Definition of
 144	\(\Div^{\,n}_{X/S}\).
